{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-83598",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "At a given point, a function $g(x,y,z)$ decreases most rapidly in the direction opposite to that of its gradient.\n\nSo, we compute $-∇g(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at the point $(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-∇g(x,y,z) & =-⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z)),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+z))⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨{y}^{2}-\\frac{4}{x+z},\\,2xy,\\,-\\frac{4}{x+z}⟩ \\\\ -∇g(1,1,1) & =-⟨{1}^{2}-\\frac{4}{1+1},\\,2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1,\\,-\\frac{4}{1+1}⟩ \\\\  & =-⟨-1,\\,2,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the function decreases most rapidly in the direction of $⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2⟩.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
